// EXPECT: E0252 — the named-argument and default-value rules (ADR-0053 §2, §3)
//
// Six refusals, each with its own note, because each is a different mistake:
//
//   * a **non-literal default**. This is the one worth explaining rather than merely refusing: the
//     value must be a literal because const-eval runs *downstream* of signature resolution
//     (ADR-0018 §3), so a signature cannot depend on a constant whose own type depends on
//     signatures. A reader told only "unsupported" would try `= 2 + 3` next, which is why the note
//     names the reason;
//   * a **positional argument after a named one**, refused so that a positional argument's meaning
//     never depends on which names preceded it — otherwise reading a call would require knowing the
//     signature and counting;
//   * a parameter supplied **twice**;
//   * a parameter with **no argument and no default**;
//   * a named argument naming a parameter that **does not exist**, with a near-name suggestion — the
//     same machinery E0212 and E0218 use (ADR-0031 §1), because a misspelling is what it is for;
//   * a default on a **`#foreign`** parameter, whose call sites are the C function's and not Jairs's
//     to fill in.
//
// Filed under `imports/invalid/` rather than `type-errors/`: these are sema diagnostics and so would
// qualify there, but that harness asserts one exact code set per file and six refusals of one code
// are clearer together than split across six files.
SIZE :: 4;
libc :: #system_library "c";

f :: (a: s64, b: s64 = 2, c: s64 = 3) -> s64 {
    return a * 100 + b * 10 + c;
}

/// A positional argument cannot follow a named one.
bad_order :: () -> s64 {
    return f(a = 1, 2);
}

/// `a` is filled positionally and then named.
bad_duplicate :: () -> s64 {
    return f(1, a = 2);
}

/// `a` has no default, so omitting it is an error however the rest are supplied.
bad_missing :: () -> s64 {
    return f(b = 2);
}

/// No such parameter. `colur` is close enough to nothing here for a suggestion; the *suggestion*
/// path is exercised by `draw` below, whose `colour` is one edit away.
bad_unknown :: () -> s64 {
    return f(1, nope = 2);
}

draw :: (colour: s64 = 1) -> s64 {
    return colour;
}

/// A misspelling one edit from a real parameter, so the `did you mean` help fires.
bad_spelling :: () -> s64 {
    return draw(colur = 2);
}

/// A default that is a named constant rather than a literal.
bad_const_default :: (n: s64 = SIZE) -> s64 {
    return n;
}

/// A default that is a constant *expression*, refused for the same reason.
bad_expr_default :: (n: s64 = 2 + 3) -> s64 {
    return n;
}

/// A `#foreign` procedure cannot carry a default.
bad_foreign :: (fd: s64 = 1, count: s64) -> s64 #foreign libc "read";
